**VIBE CODING PLAYBOOK**

Non-Tech Edition: How to Work With AI Without Breaking Things

*Kyle Rosebrook  •  Staff Engineer, AppSec **&** Platform  •  March 2026*

I didn’t start in tech. I came from hospitality management and retail. No computer science degree. I got into engineering through building things, breaking things, and asking a lot of questions that people with traditional backgrounds sometimes stopped asking because they assumed they already knew the answers.

That path gave me something useful: I never take AI tools for granted. When I started using Claude, ChatGPT, and eventually building with them professionally, I approached every output the same way I approached every new system I inherited — with the assumption that it had blind spots I hadn’t found yet.

I’m now a Staff Engineer focused on Application Security and Platform. I’m also my company’s AI SME, which means I spend a significant amount of time teaching colleagues across the org — from ops and client experience all the way up to senior engineers — how to use these tools well. This playbook is the non-tech version of what I teach.

You don’t need to understand how AI works under the hood. You need four habits. They’re not complicated. They’re just not obvious until someone tells you.

# **Tip 1: AI Doesn’t Know Your Situation. You Do.**

| **TIP 1: ****Provide your context explicitly** *"**The model defaults to the most common pattern it was trained on. Your situation is almost never the most common pattern.**"* |
| --- |

Here’s the thing about AI tools that most people find out the hard way: they sound confident regardless of whether they’re right. They’re trained on enormous amounts of general information. What they don’t have is your specific context — your company, your clients, your systems, your constraints.

When you ask an AI a question without giving it that context, it fills the gap with whatever the most common answer looks like. That answer might be totally reasonable in the abstract. It might be completely wrong for your situation.

|  | **What this looks like in practice:** I was teaching a colleague how to use Claude for client communication. She pasted in a client question and hit send without any context about who the client was, what product they were using, or what the actual issue had been. The response was polished, professional, and addressed a completely different problem than the one on the table. The AI had no way to know. She hadn’t told it. |
| --- | --- |

The fix is simple: front-load your context. Before you ask the question, give the tool the background it needs.

- **Who is the audience? **(client, internal team, leadership)

- **What’s the situation? **(complaint, question, project update, decision needed)

- **What’s the constraint? **(tone, length, what’s off-limits, what’s already been said)

- **What does success look like? **(what should happen after this is read)

The more specific you are upfront, the less time you spend editing afterward. This is not a workaround. It’s how the tool is designed to work.

# **Tip 2: Think First. Then Prompt.**

| **TIP 2: ****Form your own answer before asking** *"**If you read the AI’s output first, you’ll edit it. If you write your rough version first, you’ll actually evaluate it.**"* |
| --- |

This is the habit most people skip. They open Claude or ChatGPT, type the question, read the answer, and start editing. The problem is that once you’ve read a response — even a mediocre one — it shapes how you think. Psychologists call this anchoring. You end up making the AI’s version better rather than asking whether the AI understood the problem correctly in the first place.

The better workflow: spend sixty seconds sketching your own rough answer first. What would you write if you were doing this yourself? What’s the key point that needs to land? What’s the tone? Now prompt. Now compare.

|  | **What this looks like in practice:** This is one of the first habits I build into AI training sessions. I ask people to write a one-sentence answer to their own question before they prompt. Even a bad one-sentence answer is useful — it surfaces what you actually know, what context you’re assuming, and what the real question underneath the question is. The AI then becomes a collaborator, not an authority. |
| --- | --- |

What to look for when you compare:

- **Did the AI answer a slightly different version of your question?**

- **Did it make assumptions about context you didn’t provide?**

- **Is the tone right for your actual audience?**

- **Does it include something you know is wrong or off-base?**

Those gaps are the places where your judgment is needed. That’s the value you bring that the tool doesn’t have. Don’t give that up by skipping the sixty seconds.

# **Tip 3: Ask the Questions It Won’t Ask Itself**

| **TIP 3: ****Prompt for what could go wrong** *"**AI is optimized to be helpful. It will answer your question. It will not tell you the three ways your question has a hidden problem.**"* |
| --- |

AI tools are built to be responsive and helpful. That’s also their weakness in certain situations. They answer what you asked. They don’t proactively surface risks, edge cases, or problems you didn’t mention. That’s your job.

This matters most when the output is going somewhere consequential — to a client, into a process, into a decision. The AI has no stake in whether the thing actually works. You do.

|  | **What this looks like in practice:** When I’m reviewing AI-generated documentation or processes, I always ask one additional question after I get the output: “What’s missing from this that would matter if something went wrong?” Ninety percent of the time, the model surfaces something genuinely useful that wasn’t in the original response. It doesn’t volunteer that information. You have to ask for it. |
| --- | --- |

Questions to add to your workflow before using any significant AI output:

- **What could go wrong if this is wrong?**

- **Who else does this affect that I haven’t mentioned?**

- **Is there something specific to our situation that this doesn’t account for?**

- **If I had to push back on this, what would I push back on?**

You don’t have to ask all of them every time. Pick the one that’s most relevant to what you’re building. The habit is asking at least one before you ship the output.

# **Tip 4: Review as You Go, Not All at Once**

| **TIP 4: ****Check each step before building the next one** *"**A bad assumption at step one compounds through every step that follows. Catching it early costs thirty seconds. Catching it at the end costs everything.**"* |
| --- |

The most common mistake I see with AI-generated work is the batch review problem. Someone asks for a full document, a full plan, or a full email. They read the whole thing at the end, find something off in the first paragraph, and now have to untangle everything that built on that assumption.

The fix is incremental review. Break the task into sections or stages. After each one, check it before you move forward. This is slightly slower upfront and significantly faster overall.

|  | **What this looks like in practice:** This is the same principle behind how good engineers build software — test as you build, not after everything is assembled. I apply the same logic to any AI-assisted work I’m responsible for. Section by section. Claim by claim. If something is off, I catch it before it becomes the foundation for the next thing. |
| --- | --- |

What incremental review looks like in practice:

- **Drafting an email: **Read the opening paragraph before you read the rest. Is the tone right? Is the framing accurate? Fix it before the body is built on it.

- **Summarizing a document: **Check the first summary point against the source. If it’s off, the rest will be too.

- **Building a plan: **Validate step one makes sense before the AI scaffolds steps two through ten.

- **Researching a topic: **Spot-check one specific claim before you build on it. One hallucination can cascade through a whole deck.

# **Quick Reference: Before You Send**

Run this before any significant AI-generated output leaves your hands.

| **Check** | **Why It Matters** |
| --- | --- |
| **Did I give it my context?** | Generic context = generic output. Your situation is always more specific than the default. |
| **Did I think first?** | Your instinct tells you what matters. The AI’s output tells you what’s common. You need both. |
| **Did I ask what could go wrong?** | AI won’t surface risks unprompted. One extra question saves real problems downstream. |
| **Did I check section by section?** | Errors compound. Catching them early costs seconds. Catching them late costs hours. |
| **Am I the one responsible for this?** | AI lowers the barrier to output. It doesn’t lower the responsibility for it. |

  

Nobody taught me these habits in a classroom. I learned them by watching what broke, figuring out why, and building the checks that prevented it from breaking the same way twice.

AI doesn’t make you less responsible for your work. It makes it faster to produce work you’re responsible for. The four habits above are how you make sure speed doesn’t become a liability.

